From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:41:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] kodi: queue Dolby Vision flips instead of waiting for them
 (off)

Every Dolby Vision picture is committed with a blocking atomic commit.
The ioctl waits on the render thread for the engine's fence on the
buffer and then for the vertical blank, so the engine's asynchronous
submission buys no overlap: Kodi's work on a picture and the GPU's must
together fit in one refresh. On an N150 that shows 60p Dolby Vision at
about 40 pictures a second.

With DV_PRIVATE_NONBLOCK=1 a picture of a stable scanout (the case that
already commits without ALLOW_MODESET) is committed with
DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_NONBLOCK and an out-fence. The kernel waits for the
buffer's implicit fence and the vertical blank off the render thread,
and the next picture is prepared meanwhile. What that takes:

- A third scanout buffer and a queued state in the slot logic
  (dv_scanout_slots_queue / _flipped): a picture is written while one
  buffer is on screen and one is on its way there. Slot 2 is leased
  only then; without queued flips the two buffers alternate as before.
- One flip at a time. The window waits for the queued flip's out-fence
  before it commits the next picture, so the wait is the back-pressure
  and nothing spins. Should the kernel still answer EBUSY, the same
  request is committed blocking, which waits for the earlier flip in
  the kernel; that picture counts as shown, not queued.
- A fixed cadence. A picture is committed one refresh after its
  preparation began: the completion of the queued flip is that refresh,
  and without a queued flip the next vertical blank is waited for. Each
  picture therefore takes the same two refreshes from the render
  manager's choice to the screen, whatever the GPU's speed, and
  GetFrameLatencyAdjustment() tells the render manager so (one refresh
  more latency), which keeps audio and video where they were.
- Frames in flight: with a flip queued, the newest submitted frame is
  the one still being rendered, so only the one before it is waited for
  when two are in flight.
- The adapter learns that a queued flip completed from the DRM layer's
  flip numbers and only then lets the slots call its buffer the one on
  screen. A picture held as a repeat is compared against the newest
  buffer the display has taken, queued or shown.

Everything that is not a stable picture commits as it always did, after
the queued flip has completed: the first commit, a pending modeset, an
HDR10 signal change, a rejected commit's successor, the restore, the
menu and the startup signal, and ordinary pages. HDR10+ output is never
queued, because a picture's packet takes its statistics from the
picture before it and queueing would put that two pictures back.
With the switch on, strict commits also leave out the TEST_ONLY
commit before the real one: a refused test and a refused commit are
handled alike and neither changes the display.

The DRM layer counts queued flips, those that completed one refresh
after the one before (from the out-fences' timestamps) and later ones,
blocking commits, EBUSY answers and wait timeouts, and logs the counts
every 600 flips; DV_PRIVATE_FLIP_TRACE=1 logs every flip.

This cannot be tested without a display. It ships off: with
DV_PRIVATE_NONBLOCK unset or 0 no commit carries NONBLOCK, the test
commit stays, two scanout buffers are made and the slots never queue.
